<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Mount Lebanon Mutasarrifate</h1><p class="st-meta">1861 CE – 1918 CE · capital Baabda</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An autonomous Ottoman district of Mount Lebanon under a Christian governor and European guarantee, the core of the later Lebanese state.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the 1860 civil war, the Ottoman government and five European powers agreed in 1861 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reglement-organique-mount-lebanon">Reglement Organique</a> that united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> as a single autonomous district. It was governed by a non-Lebanese Ottoman Catholic mutasarrif appointed with the powers' consent.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The first mutasarrif was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garabet-artin-davoud-pasha">Daud Pasha</a>, an Armenian Catholic. An administrative council represented the religious communities in proportion, an early form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon's</a> confessional system. Feudal privileges were abolished and the mountain enjoyed decades of peace. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">Silk</a> production for French mills flourished, schools multiplied and emigration to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> grew. The district lacked <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/beirut">Beirut</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa</a>, which remained in Ottoman provinces.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1915 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djemal-pasha">Jamal Pasha</a> ended the autonomy; wartime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> and famine killed a large part of the population. French troops arrived in 1918, and in 1920 the mountain became the heart of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/greater-lebanon">Greater Lebanon</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mount-lebanon-mutasarrifate.jpg" alt="Mount Lebanon Mutasarrifate" width="320" height="467"><figcaption>Image: Mount Lebanon local government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Mount_Lebanon_Sanjak_Circa_1900.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1861 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Baabda</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Daud Pasha, Rustem Pasha, Ohannes Pasha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maronite Christianity, Druze, Eastern Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mutasarrifate of Mount Lebanon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1890&amp;focus=mount-lebanon-mutasarrifate" aria-label="Mount Lebanon Mutasarrifate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5592 951 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mount Lebanon Mutasarrifate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5912 1207L5709 970M5912 1207L5905 1205"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5905 1205h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5912 1207h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5912 1207h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1890&amp;focus=mount-lebanon-mutasarrifate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
